    "body": "Read this page if you are a parent or guardian setting up Grimoire for a child, or a DM with a young player at your table. If one of your players is under 16, they don't sign up on their own. A parent\nor guardian sets up the account for them, or approves a request the young\nplayer sends. That approval is what stands in for consent: nothing about the\naccount works until a parent has agreed to it.\n\nA young player's account plays like any other: they can join campaigns, play\na character, chat and take notes. It cannot use AI features, buy anything, or\nreceive email, and it never appears anywhere with the child's real email\naddress, because it doesn't have one.\n\n## Adding a young player from Account → Family\n\n1. Open the account menu, then **Account**, and in the **Family** section click\n   **Manage family →**.\n2. Click **Add a young player**.\n3. Fill in a **Display name**, a **Login name** (3 to 30 lowercase letters, numbers or\n   hyphens: this is what they'll type to sign in), and a **Password** (at least 8\n   characters, typed twice). You set both, and you can change the password later\n   from the same page.\n4. Answer the birth month and year question. Anyone under 16 gets a young\n   player's account; 16 and over signs up for themselves instead.\n5. Read the consent line and tick the box. It covers what the account can and\n   can't do, and that characters and notes a young player writes are treated\n   as ordinary game content, so a Dungeon Master running AI features in a\n   campaign they play in may include them. Real photos of a real child still\n   need your separate permission, and are never covered by this checkbox.\n6. Click **Add young player**.\n\nThe new account shows up on the Family page straight away, with the login\nname and the date it becomes theirs.\n\n## Approving a request that arrived by email\n\nSometimes a young player gets there first: they try to sign up, or answer\n\"under 16\" when Grimoire asks their age, and the app asks for a parent's\nemail address instead of letting them continue. You'll get an email with a\nlink. Open it while signed in to your own account (or sign in first if you\nneed to), and you'll land on the same \"Add a young player\" form, already\nknowing whether it's a brand-new account or one the child already started,\nand which campaign they were trying to join, if any. Fill in the login name\nand password, tick the consent box, and submit exactly as above.\n\nThat link only works for the email address it was sent to, so make sure\nyou're signed in with that same address before you approve it. If the link\nhas expired, ask the young player to try again.\n\n## Signing in with a login name\n\nA young player's account signs in with the login name you chose, not an\nemail address. On the sign-in screen, the \"Email or login name\" field takes\neither: type the login name there, and their password. There's nothing to\nconfuse them: whatever a family types is figured out automatically from\nwhether it looks like an email.\n\n## What the account can't do\n\n- **No AI.** Every AI tool is switched off for a young player's own account,\n  whether they're playing or running their own campaign.\n- **No purchases.** Grimoire Pro, credit packs and anything else that goes\n  through checkout are unreachable.\n- **No calendar feed.** A young DM's campaign can't publish the subscribable\n  calendar link other campaigns can.\n- **No email.** The account has no address of its own, so it never receives\n  notification emails, and nobody can email it by mistake.\n\nEverything else, playing a character, chatting, taking notes, running a\ncampaign within the account's limits, works the same as any other account.\n\n## Limits\n\nA young player's account has the standard limits on how much it can hold\n(campaigns, NPCs, notes and so on). If you are on Pro, your young players get\nthe same limits as your plan, for up to five accounts: the five you created\nfirst. Only the limits are shared. AI features and purchases stay off for a\nyoung player's account whatever your plan is.\n\nIf your Pro plan ends, those accounts go back to the standard limits. Nothing\nis deleted. A young player with more campaigns than the limit is asked which\none to keep active, and the rest are archived.\n\n## Resetting the password, downloading data, deleting the account\n\nAll three live on the Family page, on the card for that young player:\n\n- **Reset password** sets a new password immediately; there's no \"forgot\n  password\" email flow for an account with no email.\n- **Download their data** builds the same kind of export you can request for\n  your own account: their profile, campaign memberships, journals, notes and\n  preferences, and a list of files stored under their account.\n- **Delete account** removes the account permanently. This is also how you\n  withdraw consent: there's no separate \"unlink\" button, because once consent\n  is withdrawn there's nothing left to manage. This cannot be undone.\n\nYou can't delete your own account while you still manage an active young\nplayer's account. Remove theirs first, or wait until it becomes their own (see\nbelow).\n\n## What happens at 16\n\nNothing needs doing. From the first of the month after their 16th birthday,\nthe account stops counting as a young player's account and every restriction\nlifts on its own. The account then behaves like any other: the next time they\nsign in, Grimoire asks them to accept the current Terms of Service themselves,\nthe way any account does after a Terms update. It no longer appears on your\nFamily page.\n\n## Adding a young player to your campaign\n\nThere's no separate step for this. Send them an ordinary invite link from\n**Campaign Settings → Members & Invites**, the same way you would for any\nplayer. Have them open it while signed in to their own account. If they\nalready have a young player's account, that's it, they join like anyone else.\nIf they don't have an account yet and try to sign up from the invite link,\nGrimoire asks their age and routes them to the parent-request flow above,\ncarrying the invite along so approving the account also joins the campaign.\n\n## Approving who your young player plays with\n\nA young player can't simply drop into someone's campaign. When a join involves\na young player, it waits as a request until each parent it concerns has said\nyes:\n\n- **Your young player wants to join a campaign.** You are asked, unless you\n  run that campaign yourself.\n- **Someone wants to join your young player's campaign.** You are asked,\n  unless that someone is you.\n- **Both are young players.** Each parent is asked. If they are siblings, one\n  yes from you covers both sides.\n\nRequests waiting on you appear at the top of the Family page, under **Waiting\non you**. Each one says who wants to join which campaign and who runs it. Click\n**Approve** to let them in, or **Decline** to turn the request down. A declined\nplayer isn't told why and can ask again with the invite link. If the other\nparent still has to answer, the request shows \"You said yes. Waiting for the\nother parent.\" until they do.\n\nUntil a request is approved, the player has no access to the campaign at all.\n\nEach young player's card on the Family page also lists the campaigns they are\nat, and who sits at each table. Young players are marked, and so are you. From\nthere you can take your young player out of any campaign they play in, and you\ncan remove anyone (except the person running it) from a campaign your young\nplayer runs. Removing someone detaches their characters from the campaign but\ndoesn't delete them. The person who runs a campaign can't be removed here.\n\n## Related\n\n- [Your Account](#your-account)\n- [Inviting Players](#inviting-players)\n- [Journal, Party & Live Play](#journal-party-live-play)"
